# Commits

## Messages

Subject is the change. Start with an imperative verb. Say what changed. No
Conventional Commits type prefix (feat:, fix:). No ticket key in the subject
unless the user asks.

- Imperative: Pin, Add, Remove, Fix — not Pinned / Adds / Fixed
- Short: one line, no trailing period, no fluff
- Body only when the subject does not carry the why (migration, pin
  semantics, breaking change)

```
# bad
feat: show latest code-review outcome

# good
Show the latest code-review outcome beside Guide and Diff
```

## Branches

Prefix is one of feature/, bugfix/, hotfix/, chore/. Not feat/, fix/, or a
bare ticket key. Whole name lowercase.

After the prefix: two-digit epic, two-digit story, then a short slug. Epic
is the number on that PRD in docs/README.md Features. Story is the NN- in
docs/prds/<feature>/issues/NN-slug.md.

```
# bad
feat/Show-Latest-Outcome
feature/show-latest-code-review-outcome

# good
feature/05-01-show-latest-outcome
```

Worktree directory uses the same epic-story-slug:
.cursor/worktrees/05-01-show-latest-outcome

## Epic integration

Story branches never merge directly to develop. Each PRD owns one epic
integration branch named feature/<epic>-<prd-slug>, for example
feature/07-provider-api-automation.

Squash accepted story work onto the epic integration branch. Subject is
Merged, the epic-story pair, a colon, then the issue title:

```
# bad
Merge branch 'feature/05-01-show-latest-outcome'
feat: show latest outcome

# good
Merged 05-01: Show the latest code-review outcome beside Guide and Diff
```

A dedicated integrator combines every accepted story, runs the PRD integration
checks, and records a fixed-point review. Only then merge or fast-forward the
epic integration branch to develop.

Promote an accepted develop cut to stable main only when the user asks.
